From: EglantinePS5
Subject: [PATCH] gbb: narrow read sync, a read waits only when the GPU wrote its 64 KiB granule in the last 200 ms (GrowingBlackBeans' 0929: EDEN_GBB_READ_SYNC_PENDING=1, with EDEN_UP_AHEAD_READ_SYNC=1), off by default

Author of the change: GrowingBlackBeans (0929, public repo commit 7770e83,
patches/GrowingBlackBeans/t134/0929-gbb-read-sync-pending-and-log-cleanup.patch, written on T134
and his series). This is his read sync part applied to the T151 core unchanged (the gpu.cpp hunk
lands 17 lines higher, no other difference). His name for the switch is kept.

Switch: EDEN_GBB_READ_SYNC_PENDING=1 (= a 200 ms window) or =<ms> (20 to 2000; read once at the
first use; 0, unset, a number outside 20-2000 or text = off). Does anything only with
EDEN_UP_AHEAD_READ_SYNC=1 (0567, live) or EDEN_UP_AHEAD_READ_SYNC_AUTO (0583) arming read sync:
it narrows both.

What: read sync (0567) makes every guest read of a fence-downloaded buffer range wait while the
latest present ran ahead (its frame's fence, then a flush round trip on the GPU thread). With this
switch, such a read waits only when a GPU write queued for download at a fence
(BufferCache::QueueGpuWrite) touched the read's 64 KiB granule within the window; any other read
takes guest memory as it is, as without read sync. History, not "pending": at the start of CPU
frame C+1 the back may not have recorded frame C yet, so frame C's writes are not queued and a
pending test would miss exactly the late case; a range the GPU rewrites every frame was written in
the window anyway. Table: 65,536 slots, one per 64 KiB granule (hashed), granule and time,
lock-free (the GPU thread stores, core threads load). A collision drops the older granule, which
can only turn a wait into a skip for a granule written long ago.

Line every 10 s while on (LOG_INFO, HW_GPU): "EDEN_GBB_READ_SYNC_PENDING: last 10 s synced <n>
skipped <n> (window <ms> ms, <n> granules noted)".

Off: QueueGpuWrite tests one function-local static (the parsed window) per call; OnCPURead tests
it only inside read sync's branch, which is not reached with read sync off. Nothing is noted,
counted or logged. Changes no shader translation and no shader-store stamp: the name is in
video_core/gbb_read_sync_pending.h and gpu.cpp, outside the sources TranslationSwitches and the
translation id are made from.

Left out of his 0929: the log clean-up in gbb_flash.h and gbb_trace.h (his flash counter, flight
dumps and timelines delete their own files from other sessions). Those writers are his own series
and are not in our tree, and deleting files on the console is no part of the read sync.

Limits (his numbers, 12 Nov, his console): full read sync cleared the flicker at the house door in
6 of 6 runs but cost 24.5 -> 18.4 fps one ahead (about 13.5 ms a frame) and 26.0 -> 18.0 two ahead;
about 76 synced reads a frame, about one in a hundred found new bytes. His first impression of
this narrow form in one play session: as slow as the full one (counters not saved). The likely
reason is the design itself: the records the game reads are rewritten by the GPU every frame, so
nearly every granule read was written within 200 ms and still waits. A shorter window (=20 to =50)
skips more but then misses records written for the first time after a quiet stretch. Not covered,
as with 0567: textures the texture cache downloads at fences, query reports, reads on non-core
threads, reads after a fence wait timed out or escaped.
